 {"id":"6.8","q":"According to the book, the most appropriate way to classify a company into an industry is by:","o":["The common factors that drive its business","The name of its main product","The largest shareholder","Its stock exchange listing"],"a":0,"w":"The PVR example: classify by what drives the business."},
 {"id":"6.9","q":"Industries whose products have low income elasticity and are barely affected by economic cycles are called:","o":["Defensive industries","Deep cyclical industries","Semi-cyclical industries","Sunrise industries"],"a":0,"w":"Food, agricultural inputs and healthcare are the book's examples."},
 {"id":"6.10","q":"According to the book, which industry is an example of a deep cyclical industry?","o":["Capital goods","Healthcare","Food","Agricultural inputs"],"a":0,"w":"Capital goods and steel swing hard with economic and commodity cycles."},
 {"id":"6.11","q":"The consumer durables industry has exhibited the traits of a ______ industry.","o":["Semi-cyclical","Defensive","Deep cyclical","Declining"],"a":0,"w":"It grows in expansion and falls in recession but keeps a base level of demand."},
 {"id":"6.12","q":"Estimating a therapy's market by multiplying the number of patients by the average spend per patient is an example of:","o":["Top-down market sizing","Bottom-up market sizing","Value migration","SCP analysis"],"a":0,"w":"Bottom-up would add up the therapy revenue of each hospital."},
 {"id":"6.13","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the drivers of secular trends listed in the book?","o":["Quarterly inventory adjustments","Technological advancement","Demographic changes","Changes in regulation or government policy"],"a":0,"w":"Inventory adjustments drive short inventory cycles, not secular trends."},
 {"id":"6.14","q":"According to the book, the ageing population in Japan resulted in:","o":["A decrease in per capita consumption of beer","A rise in demand for western clothing","A shift to electric vehicles","Growth of call taxis"],"a":0,"w":"An example of demographic change driving a secular trend."},
 {"id":"6.15","q":"The advent of digital cameras causing the decline of the film roll industry is an example of:","o":["Cross-industry value migration","Geographic value migration","Migration across the value chain","Seasonal trend"],"a":0,"w":"One industry gained at the cost of another."},
 {"id":"6.16","q":"Falling mobile service prices hurt Indian telecom companies but helped digital content providers. This is value migration:","o":["Across the value chain","Across geographies","Across companies in the same industry","Across economic cycles"],"a":0,"w":"Value moved between different levels of the value chain."},
 {"id":"6.17","q":"In which stage of the business life cycle have most potential customers already adopted the product?","o":["Mature","Pioneering","Growth","Reinvention"],"a":0,"w":"In the mature stage, few new customers are left."},
 {"id":"6.18","q":"Porter's five forces model was developed in:","o":["1979","1969","1989","1999"],"a":0,"w":"By Dr. Michael Porter in 1979."},
 {"id":"6.19","q":"As per the book, which of the following are the vertical forces in Porter's model?","o":["Bargaining power of suppliers and bargaining power of customers","Threat of substitutes and threat of new entrants","Threat of established rivals and threat of substitutes","Threat of new entrants and bargaining power of suppliers"],"a":0,"w":"The book lists 3 horizontal forces (substitutes, new entrants, rivals) and 2 vertical ones (suppliers, customers)."},
 {"id":"6.20","q":"As per the book, which of these industries is termed unattractive under Porter's model?","o":["Aviation","Education","FMCG","IT"],"a":0,"w":"Aviation, telecom, retail, textile, sugar and power are termed unattractive."},
